  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThrough Tuesday: Continued dry and slightly warmer through the \nperiod. Mid afternoon satellite imagery/RAP analysis showed the \ncirculation around an upper level trough over the southern \nAppalachians and a broad mid level high extending from the desert \nsouthwest into the southern Pains. Our CWA will sit between these \nfeatures through Tuesday while a surface high continues to ridge \nacross our region from the north. This will continue to limit return \nmoisture and allow a gradual warming trend to continue. Although \ntemperatures will top out slightly warmer than normal Tuesday, \nrelative humidities will remain low enough to mitigate heat stress \nconcerns. /22/ \n\nTomorrow night through next Monday: \n\nTroughing will slowly advance east across the Deep South while \ngradually weakening through mid-week. This will allow high pressure \naloft to strengthen across the Lower Mississippi River Valley during \nthe mid and latter portion of the forecast period.  As a result, the \nweather across the forecast area will remain rather quiet, with rain \nchances very low through mid week and only rising into the 20-30% \nranges for most as we move into Friday and the weekend. Rain chances \nlikely come in the form of diurnal, afternoon and evening isolated \nshowers and storms that wane overnight. Strong to severe storms are \nnot expected though with the increasing heat and humidity, a briefly \ngusty thunderstorm or two wouldn't be out of the question. \n\nAs far as impactful weather during the forecast period, this will \nagain come in the form of increasing heat stress, mainly from \nThursday through Sunday.  The slightly drier air currently residing \nacross the CWA was been a nice and welcomed relief from the \noppressively humid conditions over the past week.  Unfortunately \nthrough the week, moisture will gradually increase as dew points \nclimb back well into the 70s areawide heading into the weekend. \nThis, as high temperatures climb back into at least the middle 90s, \nwill yield afternoon heat index values approaching levels where \nmultiple days of perhaps a \"Heat Advisory\" will need to be hoisted \nfor portions of, if not all of, the forecast area during the \nThursday through Monday timeframe and perhaps beyond.  This will \ncontinue to be monitored in future forecasts. /86/",
